{"url": "https://www.ccny.cuny.edu/bme", "final_url": "https://www.ccny.cuny.edu/bme", "status": 200, "method": "chromium", "fetched": "2026-11-13", "text": "Skip to main content\nSEARCH\nLOGIN\nABOUT\nACADEMICS\nADMISSIONS\nRESEARCH\nSTUDENT AFFAIRS\nSUPPORT CCNY\nBIOMEDICAL ENGINEERING\nHOME\nWHAT'S NEW?\nPEOPLE\nACADEMICS\nRESEARCH\nABOUT US\nBME ALBUM AND EVENTS\nHome\n/\nThe Grove School of Engineering\n/\nBiomedical Engineering\nBIOMEDICAL ENGINEERING AT CITY COLLEGE: ENGINEERING THE HEALTHCARE OF NEW YORK\nBME @ CCNY is committed to transforming the lives of New Yorkers through engineering and medicine\nBME @ CCNY is the only public biomedical engineering program in New York City\nWe serve and educate New Yorkers while making a national impact through our research\nWe collaborate with world-leading hospitals in NYC to make our research a reality\nWe exemplify CCNY's mission of diversity, inclusion, and economic mobility\nWe provide affordable, world-class education for all in the heart of New York City\nBME @ CCNY welcomes two new faculty members in 2027!\nDr. Kelsey DeFrates is a bioengineering and biomaterials specialist, with PhD training at the University of California, Berkeley and post-doctoral training at the University of California, San Francisco. She has received many honors to date, including a Goldwater Scholarship while an undergraduate student at Rowan University, and an NSF Graduate Research Fellowship. Dr. DeFrates has a strong record of accomplishments in the areas of biomaterials and designing materials for drug delivery, as well as work on understanding bacterial biomechanics and mechanobiology, and their role in disease. At CCNY Dr. DeFrates will focus on developing a novel research program that leverages her background in biomaterials engineering and drug delivery to design materials that control the release of probiotic organisms to treat diseases. This cutting-edge approach in 'living materials,' composites made with living cells and non-living scaffolds, has many applications in biomedical areas as well as more broadly in environmental and sustainability areas. Dr. DeFrates is currently completing an NIH F32 Postdoctoral Fellowship grant and will join the BME Department in 2027.\nDr. Kevin Costa has a distinguished record in research, mentoring, and teaching, having been with the Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai in Surgery and Cardiology since 2009, after starting his career in 1999 as a founding faculty in the Department of Biomedical Engineering at Columbia University. He has made seminal scientific contributions in complementary areas including: 1) Cardiac tissue and organoid engineering for human disease modeling and therapeutic discovery; 2) Atomic force microscopy for microscale cell and tissue mechanobiology and disease mechanisms; and 3) Computational modeling of cardiac mechanics and advanced manufacturing of bioinspired metamaterials for cardiac repair. Dr. Costa has been funded by the NIH as principal investigator or co-investigator continuously since 2002 and has had considerable other research funding over the years (NSF CAREER, Whitaker Foundation, Additional Ventures). Dr. Costa has substantial translational research experience and is the co-founder and CSO of Novoheart US Corp., which created the first human cardiac tissue engineering technology with data accepted by the FDA for ongoing first-in-human gene therapy trials for heart failure. Dr. Costa will help expand the BME department\u2019s expertise in cardiovascular engineering and entrepreneurship, and will also strengthen CCNY's related research in biomaterials, nanotechnology, mechanobiology, and regenerative medicine.\nCCNY Hosts Neuromodulation Conference Showcasing Advances in Brain Science and Medical Technology\nCCNY hosted the 2026 NYC Neuromodulation Conference July 31 - Aug. 2, bringing together an international community of scientists, engineers, clinicians, and industry leaders to explore advances in brain stimulation and medical technology. Founded in 2013 and co-chaired by Professor Marom Bikson, the conference celebrated its largest meeting to date, highlighting innovations in brain-computer interfaces, artificial intelligence, wearable devices, and electrical, magnetic, and ultrasound neuromodulation. A special focus was placed on emerging applications in women's health, including research by BME PhD student Catarina Ferraz investigating how noninvasive brain stimulation may influence reproductive hormone regulation. Another highlight was High-Capacity transcranial Direct Current Stimulation (HC-tDCS), developed by BME PhD student Kyle Donnery, Dr. Mohammad FallahRad (recent BME PhD grad), and collaborators, which recently advanced to an initial clinical study of accelerated depression treatment. The conference underscored CCNY's leadership in BME, its contributions to translating neuroscience into clinical technologies, and its role in fostering international collaborations to develop faster, safer, and more accessible treatments for neurological and psychiatric disorders.\nRecent Research Highlight\nAn AI model for breast cancer detection designed by researchers in the lab of Professor Lucas Parra was recognized by CUNY Chancellor F\u00e9lix Matos Rodr\u00edguez in his October 2025 State of the University address. Developed in collaboration with Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center, the open-source tool can detect breast cancer on MRI scans up to a year before clinical diagnosis, potentially increasing early detection rates by up to 30% in high-risk patients. Chancellor Matos Rodriguez praised the group's innovation for outperforming existing automated tools and advancing equity in health care. See CCNY Press Release for more details.\nCelebrating Our Successes\nBME @ CCNY Distinguished Professor Emeritus Sheldon Weinbaum was awarded the National Medal of Science by President Biden in October 2023. Professor Weinbaum is an elected member of the National Academy of Sciences (NAS), the National Academy of Engineering (NAE), and the National Academy of Medicine (NAM). Professor Weinbaum also received the Benjamin Franklin Medal in December 2021 and is a Fellow of the American Institute for Medical and Biological Engineering (AIMBE), the American Society of Mechanical Engineers (ASME), and the American Physiological Society (APS).\nDepartment News, Seminars, and Special Events\nRead our Newsletter\nConnect with us to Support our Mission\nWho are you and what can we do for you?\nProspective Undergrad\nPlease click here to learn more about our BME Bachelor's in Engineering (BE) degree program\nPlease click here to learn about our accelerated 4+1 option that culminates in a BE and MS in BME\nPlease click here if you are a potential transfer student\nSee what a degree in BME can do for you: check out what our former students have gone on to do!\nProspective Grad Student\nPlease click here to learn more about our MS in BME program\nPlease click here to learn more about our Master's in Translational Medicine program\nPlease click here to learn more about our BME PhD program\nSee what a degree in BME can do for you: check out what our former students have gone on to do!\nCurrent Student\nLearn more about our faculty and staff\nFind your degree requirements here: Undergrad, Master's in BME, Master's in Translational Medicine (MTM), PhD\nUndergrads, please consider our new 4+1 program -- earn your BE and MS in 5 years!\nFind a summer research opportunity here\nSee yourself in one of our research labs by connecting with our faculty\nPotential Research Collab\nPlease click here to learn more about the world-class research in BME\nPlease click here to learn more about our departmental ties to industry\nPlease click here to learn more about the New York Center for Biomedical Engineering and world-class hospital collaborations\nBME @ CCNY Alumnus\nPlease click here to see the latest updates and successes from your old home\nOur current students want to network with you! Please check out the LinkTree of the BMES student chapter\nConnect with us on our LinkedIn group for Alumni!\nPlease complete this form to update your contact info with us. If you can, please also consider giving back to support our current students\nCONTACT INFORMATION\nSteinman Hall, Room ST-401\nMailing Address: 160 Convent Avenue\nPhysical Address: 275 Convent Avenue (at W. 140th St)\nNew York, NY 10031\nFor more information about the BME B.E. program contact:\nDr. Phillip Payton, Administrative Director/Undergraduate Advisor, 212.650.5283, bme@ccny.cuny.edu\nFor more information about the BME M.S. program contact:\nProf. Bingmei Fu, Master's Advisor, 212.650.7531, bfu@ccny.cuny.edu\nFor more information about the Master's in Translational Medicine (MTM) program contact:\nProf. Chris Fritton, MTM Program Director, 212.650.5612, jfritton@ccny.cuny.edu\nFor more information about the BME Ph.D. program contact:\nProf. Lucas Parra, Ph.D. Advisor, 212.650.7211, bmephd@ccny.cuny.edu\nFor general information about the BME Department contact:\nMs. Patricia Cupid, Administrative Specialist, 212.650.6707, pcupid@ccny.cuny.edu\nProf. Susannah Fritton, Department Chair, 212.650.5213, fritton@ccny.cuny.edu\nWe use cookies and other tracking technologies to improve your browsing experience on our website, analyze our website traffic, and understand where our visitors are coming from. If you choose \"ACCEPT ALL\", you consent to the use of all cookies. You can accept and reject individual cookie types and revoke your consent for the future at any time at \"Settings\".\nPrivacy policy\nAbout us\nCOOKIE SETTINGS\nDENY ALL\nACCEPT ALL\nThe City College of New York\n160 Convent Avenue New York, NY 10031\np: 212.650.7000\nSTUDENTS\nEmail\nAcademic Calendar\nCourses - CCNY Bulletin\nCourses - CUNY list\nApply Now - Undergraduate\nApply Now - Graduate\nLibraries\nBenny's Campus Store\nRegistrar\nStudent Employment\nFACULTY & STAFF\nWebmail\nDirectory\n